Date: Wednesday, 8 September 1993 16:00:00 PST8
From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

It is often argued that rights derive from a social
contract:  I agree not to use violence on you, if you will
agree not to use violence on me.

The social contract theory is probably valid for systems
of justice and judgment, and details of property rights
and the like, but rights cannot be derived solely from
contract, or solely from custom, because contract assumes a
pre-existing principle that contracts should be honored,
that they should be for value, and so on and so forth.  A
similar problem exists for customs.

These pre-existing principles are called natural law. 
They are rules that we intuitively and rationally recognize
as necessary and right, for reasons that I discuss
extensively in ftp.netcom.com:/pub/jamesd/rights.

If you argue rights from contract, then the nihilists at
Loompanics will correctly argue that there is no contract
to honor contract, therefore anything is permitted,
including rape and torture, and the democratic
totalitarians such as Rousseau will correctly argue that any
and all social contracts are equally permissible, including
those that are not for value and are changed capriciously,
therefore the majority should exercise limitless and
absolute power, and the totalitarian absolutists, Hobbes
and the Fascists, will correctly argue that there is no
social contract because no one consented to it, therefore
the only way people can live in peace together is for a
single man to define good and evil according to his
arbitrary and absolute will.

To argue that the social contract rests on the social
contract, as Perry does, is to build on sand.  The nature
of man provides a more solid foundation.

