Date: Thu, 9 Sep 93 9:11:09 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: The Idea of Free Speech is Slipping Away

> At  3:27 PM 9/8/93 -0700, Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~ wrote:
> >Let
> >the masses _discover_ that, without crypto, they're suddenly less competitive
> >in all things. I think it'll work better.
> 
> This assumes that good ideas always win in the end (unless someone promotes
> them). I don't buy it. By the time Joe Sixpack figures this out, it'll
> likely be too late.
> 
> Maybe instead of political activism, we could try the get rich quick angle.
> *How YOU can make MEGAbucks using cryptography in your home!*
> 
> tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)

This is one of those cases where there simply isn't any basis for argument,
since it cannot be proven one way or the other, but interestingly, yes, this
is basically the assumption I am making. Of course, to a degree, it is
certainly a generalization. Any idea/concept/meme which never travels farther
than the confines of one mind for one reason or another is dependent upon
the dynamics of the single organism.

So where someone might start citing case after case of good ideas that 
never came to anything, I'd probably offer the _safe_ reply that either the
idea _wasn't_ good, or that it simply isn't being presented at the right
time, in the right manner, or whatever. Should something be done about it?
I would say no, because I feel that the _reason_ good ideas get burried is
due to the dynamics of this idea/meme market out there. So, take any allegedly
good idea which never made it far. I would propose that had it actually
propogated at that time, instead of being stifled, it might have been more
destructive than good. See, an idea in itself is nothing without context.
Both the content and the context of an idea are key to its success.

I know I'm belabouring the point, but I felt an example might be necessary.
Take one of the extropian principles, "boundless expansion", and more
specifically, the subset of that which addresses specifically the endless
expansion into space. One might claim that, at any cost, expanding into
space is simply a necessity, and should have received every bit of effort
mankind was capable of giving it since the dawn of time. In other words,
expanding into space is just a "good idea". But I would claim that this
absolute claim is nothing without being put in the proper context. Forgetting
for the moment that mankind perhaps might have been capable of space travel
100's of years ago were it not for certain political and religious
influences, let us focus instead on the latter half of this century. Let us
"pretend" for a moment that our space programs were just a few years ahead
of the schedule we now know as fact. Maybe WWII could have lasted a bit
longer, and might have prompted an even faster-paced technology ramp in a
number of areas, including space. Let us then assume that by now, we might
have, with the proper attention and focus, put a base on the moon, and today
might be mining it already. Would we be better off today? Before you answer,
think of what the implications of that might have been during the Cold War.

Maybe we could take a much less significant piece of space history, and ponder
what might have happened had the US not dropped all its heavy-lift programs
in favor of the shuttle program. Might any of these things have had major
effects on the course of recent history? I think so. WOuld it have been
positive? Maybe not. Perhaps by _pushing_ these seemingly good ideas at the
time, the world political climate might have taken a turn for the worse, and
the space program today might be a barely-flickering afterthought. _WAIT_,
what am I saying? I don't need to use silly, hypothetical examples, what
about the shuttle program? A good many, intelligent people pushed _that_
idea _very_ hard, in the name of progress, and look what _it_ has done to
the US space program. I get the willies just thinking about all the neet
things we might have been doing in the late 70's, and the 80's, had we not
put all our eggs in _that_ basket.

Does that mean the shuttle was then a bad idea? I don't think so. I just
think it was pushed too hard and for the wrong reasons. Could the same
kind of thing happen with the technologies and ideas we discuss here? I think
it definitely could, and probably would. Push crypto too fast, and you might
set it back by 50 years by provoking major Statist action worldwide. WHo
knows. Same with nanotech. I can see it now, a UN ban on all nanotech research
for fear of creating, what is it, grey goo?

Likewise, I would disagree with whoever it was (sorry, I read some 500-1000
messages in email today) that claimed the LP at least propogated the liberty
meme, or some such thing. I would argue that this was a _very_ short term
benefit, and that instead, in the long run, the LP has made liberty more
unpopular if anything. What, instead, is more popular today? Ross Perot, of
course. Does Ross stand for liberty? Heck no. I voted for him because I think
he was far the lesser of many evils, and might actually quicken the big setup
for government to wither. But in fact, Ross has everyone I know ready and
willing to _sacrifice_ individual liberties and freedom in the name of the
State. So you see, it only took _one man_ to undo the allegedly good, solid
work of the LP, and make liberty and freedom an _unpopular_ concept.

Well, I've said enough. Didn't write any other mail after reading that large
mass of it in my box this morning, so I guess my word-quota for the morning
spilled out all over this one list. Sorry.

Tony Hamilton
