Normally, I don't see James Donald's stuff, but this was quoted in
someone else's message.

Its also nonsense.

There needn't be a "pre-existing principle" for contracts. The clear
and simple understanding of what it is that two individuals are
agreeing to is sufficient. If one breaks the agreement, the other has
a range of options, from beating the crap out of the other guy to
simply failing to ever cooperate with him again.

Rights are easy to derive solely from custom and contract -- indeed,
contracts are derived solely from custom. Claiming that you need to
have contract law arrive out of the sky fully formed, springing from
the head of Zeus, is as silly as claiming that life couldn't have
evolved because DNA is expressed via rules and without having
ribosomes pre-existing to interpret those rules you couldn't have
created life. Geesh.

People agree to the notion of "rights" based on a simple mutual
agreement -- respect mine and I'll respect yours, and once you cease
to abide by these rules I won't abide by them either and we'll have
warfare on our hands. There is no need for a "principle" that the
agreement needs to be honored -- its self executing. Break it and you
lose all the benefits (peace and having your rights honored) that you
made it for -- keep it and you get all the benefits. You needn't have
an absolute notion of contract law to decide which way to go -- you
need merely be a creature that evolution has designed to go for the
path that assures survival.

