Ratha asks about digital cash:

> I know close to nothing about the ways of cypherpunks, and since reading
> many of these posts have had my curiosity sparked in a big way. I've
> been searching for a back issue of Wired which I think had an article on
> crypto-anarchy, but with no luck. Can anyone point me to some
> enlightening literature, preferably online?

The issue of "Wired" you are looking for is #2, or 1.2 in their
numbering scheme. It came out in the spring and is now out of print,
with back issues going for some huge amount ("Wired" is now a major
publishing success, so back issues are priced for collectors,
apparently).

I'm one of the "Crypto Rebels" on the cover, the one on the left in
fact. They had us wear theatrical (white) masks, for an artistic
point, but our normal faces are shown inside. Steven Levy, who wrote
the book "Hackers" (1984), and also "Artificial Life" (1992),
interviewed several of the Cypherpunks and wrote the article. A very
fine job, but I may be biased. (Other good pieces include Kevin
Kelly's article on crypto in "Whole Earth Review," Summer 1993, and
Julian Dibbell's cover story on Cypherpunks in the August 3, 1993
issue of "The Village Voice.")

Crypto anarchy is a term I invented, in my 1988 "Crypto Anarchist
Manifesto," to describe the set of ideas associated with anonyomous
markets and communications, reputations, strong encryption, digital
money, digital escrow services, and the whole "colonization of
cyberspace" idea. Vernor Vinge's "True Names" was the greatest
influence, with the then-nascent "American Information Exchange"
(AMIX) leading me to speculate (to Phil Salin, Eric Drexler, others)
that the likely markets for AMIX-type systems would be the buying and
selling of corporate information (this is where 90% of knowledge
chunks now reside....will Lockheed or Apple encourage their employees
to log on to an AMIX-like system and sell corporate knowledge for
their own profit?). 

This analysis of AMIX led to "BlackNet," a hypothetical network
using crypto anarchy to facilitate the buying and selling of nearly
_anything_, including Stealth bomber blueprints, launch codes for
black market Russian nukes, stolen credit card numbers, contract
killings, and other fun stuff. Drexler called my ideas "profoundly
immoral" and Marc Stiegler averred, in 1988, that "some things are
best left unsaid." Being unpersuaded by this, and seeing crypto
anarchy as largely inevetable over the next few decades, I pushed on.

Eric Hughes and I called the first meeting of a bunch of Bay Area
software hackers to discuss these ideas. The first meeting was a year
ago, and we played "the crypto anarchy game," a paper-based
role-playing game in which anonymous remailers ("mixes") were
simulated with sealed envelopes, digital cash was simulated with play
money, and black market knowledge (formula for RU-486, production
plans for 486 chip, radar crossection of Tomahawk cruise missiles,
etc.) was similarly handled. The idea was to give a graphic immersion
in the ideas of Chaum and others, to really show these folks how it
could all work.

The meeting lasted all day and into the night, and even the next day.
A regular group was born. A mailing list was started, running on John
Gilmore's machine. Jude Milhon ("St. Jude"), an editor for "Mondo
2000," attended and, by the second meeting, had jokingly dubbed us "a
bunch of cypherpunks." The name stuck. (I've gotten used to it, and
even think the bizarreness of it helps get some attention. Names like
"Personal Privacy Club" just don't have the panache.)

Subscriptions to the mailing list: cypherpunks-request@toad.com

Onward to Ratha's specific questions:

> I'll spare you all my questions on PGP, etc., but one thing that I'd
> like to discuss is the theory behind digicash. It sounds like a
> fantastic idea, but I can't imagine how it would work. If I understand
> basic economics correctly, money must be scarce in order to be used as a
> valuable medium of exchange. So with the ease with which information can
> be copied & spread in mind, how exactly would digicash work? How would
> exchanges take place? How easy would it be to hack $50 (or $5000000?)
> Would it be subject to inflation, etc. like our present system? How
> would banking fit in? Is the whole idea worked out, or still in debate
> mode?

Money is already in several forms besides "gold" or "silver" (the
canonical things of value due to scarcity):

-Federal Reserve Notes....a promise to exchange for....for *other*
Federal Reserve Notes!! (convertibility to gold and/or silver, as
spelled out in the Constitution, was abandoned many years ago)

-coupons of various sorts are a form of money, freely exchangeable

-chits and scrip issued by local businesses, by communitites, etc.
Sometimes to support the local economy, to bypass the IRS (which takes
a dim view of untaxed barter transactions---wait 'til they see
cyberspace!), etc. (A recent issue of leftist "Mother Jones" had a
great article on these "alternative currencies")

-"bearer bonds" and other such financial instruments (Perry M., Duncan
F., and others can elaborate)

-others

The key is that people have the expectation that the thing they are
accepting as money will continue to have roughly the same value (a
vague term) until they can convert it to something else. Thus, issues
involving forgery, counterfeiting, and inflation are key. (Gold is
considered "true money"--sounds like the sequel to "True
Names"--because it is conveniently dense in value, is scarce, is easy
to detect forgeries of, and is accepted in nearly all cultures.)

A "consensual hallucination," to borrow Bill Gibson's term for
cyberspace.

Digital money can come in many flavors, of varying degrees of
sophistication:

1. Alice walks into Credit Suisse in Geneva and hands the teller
$10,000 in U.S. currency. She is given a 20-digit number, which she
can write down, load into her Newton, tattoo on her arm, remember
(!!), whatever. She flies back to Boston and transfers this number,
under whatever circumstances one might imagine, to Bob. Bob goes to
the same bank in Geneva, presents the number and is paid $10,000 in
U.S. currency.

This simplistic protocol is of course doable today, but has several
flaws. I'll list them briefly, and cite some workarounds or solutions
(some of which will point to follow-on forms):

* The number is readily duplicatable. Solution: the bank pays the
_first_ presenter. Thus, the number is like a combination to a train
locker...whoever reaches it first gets the contents. Holders of
numbers are thus likely to be very careful with the number.

* Can't Alice race to the bank and cash the number immediately after giving
it to Bob? Solution: escrow firms, which act as agents (local branch
banks) for the cashing. Thus, Bob waits for the cash to "clear"--and
he need not travel all the way to Geneva of course--and then the
number cannot be spent by Alice.

* Loss of the number. Tough shit.

2. More sophisticated forms of "digital cash" have been devised by
cryptology researchers, notably David Chaum and his students at CWI in
Amsterdam. These protocols are very hard to describe in simple words,
but involve the "transaction clearinhouses" as in the above example.

There appears to be no simple "digital coin" that is unforgeable,
can't be double-spent (without online transaction clearing), etc. How
could there be, as numbers are readily duplicated?

3. Tamper-resistant chips in smart cards are another approach, though
not ideal. (Can't use the hardware technology in cyberspace, for
example.)

In summary, no _simple_ techniques seem to exist. The protocols for
digital money are complicated, and may need to involve clearinghouses,
tangible objects (chips, certificates), etc..

The Cypherpunks group is hoping to explore these possibilities and
find the breakthoughs that may exist. Some form of "Pretty Good
Digicash," "PGD," may be coming.

-Tim May



